WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  colombians are clamoring for change  [Applause]  and gustavo petro is counting on it to  make history  the former rebel came out on top in the  first round of presidential elections  bringing him one step closer to becoming  colombia's first leftist leader in what  is traditionally a conservative country  i trust colombian society in its will to  change  in the end  there are only two options  it's a relatively simple choice  as they are in colombia  which in my opinion is more corruption  more violence  more hunger  or  we can change colombia  petrol is a former member of the m19  guerrilla movement  and a former mayor of bogota  in his presidential campaign he has  vowed profound economic and social  change  a petro presidency would be historic in  more ways than one  his running mate francia marquez is  hoping to become colombia's first ever  black female vice president  for us as a black community it is a  great recognition to see a black person  there black people have always been  enslaved we have always been at the  bottom  and to have the opportunity to be there  and represent us as black people fills  us with pride  petro's coalition fell short of the 50  percent needed for an outright victory  he will now face populist millionaire  rudolfo hernandez in a second round of  voting the man dubbed colombia's donald  trump emerged as a surprise second place  candidate  no matter who wins the runoff on june 19  change is coming  one way or the other  in colombia leftist gustavo petro has  come out on top in the first round of  presidential elections  the former guerrilla and one-time bogota  mayor  has campaigned on a platform of tackling  poverty and crime petro will now face  the independent candidate rodolfo  hernandez in a runoff the businessman  finished in a surprise second place in  the vote  let's get more now from dw correspondent  ramirez he joins me now from the capitol  bogota johan first of all tell me more  about this surprise results  yeah it's very surprising in one hand is  surprising but on the other hand it's a  clear message the colombians are tired  of the political elite here during the  last weeks we have been talking about  gustavo petro the front runner and then  fico gutierrez the right-wing candidate  who is who was supposed to be  in the second place but now the  surprises rodolfo hernandez is getting  that second place going into the second  round of this election and uh is a  message people are tired of the  political elite royal fernandez is an  independent candidate a private  entrepreneur during all his life he was  just once mayor of a little city here in  bogota otherwise he has been all his  life working by himself in a way many  people here in colombia compares compare  rodol fernandez with donald trump  because he has a more unconventional way  to do politics and  now he's at the second runner it's going  to be very interesting to see what is  going to happen the next uh june 19th  when the second round is going to take  place  you say that voters there are fed up  with political elites and and status quo  so what are some of those major issues  that colombia is facing that have  influenced this election and this  apparent outcome  this first round was marketed by  a lot of attacks between the candidates  the two main candidates it was a very  polarized um electoral campaign and i  think that people also was tired of  seeing these candidates insulting  themselves otherwise on the other hand  rodol fernandez was trying to propose  more things and less insulting the other  candidates and i think we can see that  today uh in this result it was a very  polarized  campaign in a country that has been  polarized for more than  50 years after the the during the civil  war so i think people wanted something  new different a more moderate candidate  we are not saying that royal fernand is  going to be the next president but it's  a strong message today in colombia and  is uh  so  this is shaking the political scenario  in the country now  gw correspondent johan ramirez joining  us there from bogota thanks very much

